cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use the SDSA software or the spiking self-attention ideas presented here in your work, please cite it as follows."
type: software
title: "SDSA: Spike-Driven Self-Attention"
abstract: >
  SDSA is a proof-of-concept spiking transformer: the standard transformer blueprint (patch embedding, self-attention, feed-forward blocks,
  classification head) reimplemented so that every internal nonlinearity is a leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) spiking neuron rather than a
  continuous-valued activation. Query, key, and value projections are each passed through their own LIF neuron to produce true binary
  (0.0/1.0) spike trains, and self-attention is computed directly over those spikes without a softmax. Because the spiking threshold
  (Heaviside step) has no useful gradient almost anywhere, training uses a custom surrogate-gradient torch.autograd.Function (a fast-sigmoid
  surrogate) in its backward pass. The model is validated on MNIST digit classification and is intentionally small enough to train locally
  on a single Apple Silicon MacBook Air. SDSA is a hand-written learning exercise, not a reproduction of any specific published architecture
  or a benchmark against dense transformers.
